 "measures": [],
 "notes": "A worked example of the RF designer: a Gaussian beam telescope at 150 GHz (2 mm), a corrugated feed horn’s beam through two plano-hyperbolic lenses in polypropylene into an identical receiving horn. Lens 1 was made with Design a lens: placed where the feed’s beam has a 20 dB edge taper on its 100 mm aperture (195 mm from the horn), its hyperbolic face (conic −n²) turning the beam’s phase centre into a flat wavefront. Lens 2 is its mirror image, 430 mm on (twice the phase radius), so the receiver sees the feed imaged on itself. Select a lens and press Design the RF system to open it. It couples 94.9% to 99.8% of the beam into the receiver across 135 to 165 GHz; all told 75.8% (-1.20 dB) at 150 GHz. The largest loss is reflection, -0.67 dB from the four bare faces (3.8% each for n = 1.483): at 150 GHz a printed matching layer would be coarser than a quarter wave, so Make it leaves the faces bare. Absorption takes -0.35 dB and the edges -0.10 dB. Try Analyses for the coupling over the band, Tolerance for what a millimetre of spacing costs, or Make it for the quote."
}